some songs might speak to your soul even  if you don't know the words the almost  magical way that music reflects and  influences our emotions let the American  poet Henry Wadsworth Longfellow to  declare it the universal language of  mankind but how Universal is it really a  team of more than a dozen researchers  and countless undergraduates set out to  compile and annotate descriptions and  recordings of musical performances  around the world their first finding  music is indeed universal or at least  statistical modeling strongly suggests  that more than 99% of societies have  music then they analyze the song  descriptions for 60 very different  societies from the Pawnee and plains of  North America to the sámi of Scandinavia  a representative sample that let them  test dozens of theories they looked at  different qualities related to song  performance as they classified the songs  they found that three dimensions  accounted for more than a quarter of the  variability between songs  the first was how formal or informal a  song was songs high informality had  large audiences and lots of instruments  often involved ceremonial events and  frequently had adults only audiences in  formal events had smaller audiences  including children or no audience at all  second was how arousing a song was to  its listeners lively events involving  lots of dancing were high on this  dimension while a low value reflected a  calmer event like someone singing to  themselves or a baby last was how  religious a song was songs used in  ceremonies and funerals were high in  religiosity while those without a  spiritual context scored low the  researchers applied their findings to  for widespread categories of music  lullabies dance songs love songs and  healing songs and found clear trends  most dance music was highly arousing  informal but low in religiosity  lullabies were mostly low formality and  low arousal and healing songs cord  flying all the love songs were low in  all dimensions even more interesting the  distribution of these behaviors was  similar in all societies studied next  researchers looked at underlying  acoustic features of a song like tempo  and rhythm they collected 118 recordings  of songs throughout the world  they found two factors that explained a  quarter of the variability in songs  melodic complexity and rhythmic  complexity unsurprisingly dance songs  were more rhythmic since a lullabye but  overall the four categories of songs  couldn't be told apart solely by their  melodica rhythmic complexity scholars  say that their database could fuel  future research into even more  intriguing questions about music  universals and their method might be  used to pick out patterns and other hard  to analyze fields like storytelling or  visual art who knows what other  commonalities underlie the diversity of  cultures around the world  [Music]
 

